  "worked_solution": "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{\u23de}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 20 \\\\ 1 & 30 \\\\ 1 & 40\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{\u23de}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{\u23de}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}[\\begin{aligned}750 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 200\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0.05\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.5+0.05x.$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the rope will stretch if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it, we substitute $x=65$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5+0.05(65) \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the rope will stretch $3.75$ meters if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it.\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=65$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
